# An edit in Avik's Gmail draft that he did not make (2026-11-17)

Account avikj@berkeley.edu (Google Workspace, UC Berkeley); draft "A hypercubical solution to Human Compatibility" to russell@berkeley.edu (draft id r1788587893572218005), edited in Safari on his Mac. The draft was open on no other device; he runs no writing extensions.

What he wrote (the copy he gave Claude at 06:07 UTC on the 18th): "...omit all information about the Nakba (Israels founding upon ethnic cleansing)". He later revised the sentence to name "the origin of Israel's decades-long genocide".

What he found: Claude, checking the letter, pointed to "Israels" as a typo. Searching the draft for "Israel", Avik found no match: the sentence read "the origin of a decades-long genocide". He restored "Israel's"; the draft saved at 07:15 UTC reads "(implied raised context as the origin of Israel's decades-long genocide)".

His words (2026-11-18, 07:16-07:27 UTC): "gmail edited my text to wipe this somehow"; "There was an edit which *I* did not make. I would *never* reduce blame like that after writing it correctly the first time."; "*I did not do any action on screen to change Israels to a, my eyes have been open my intention has been continuous ... I was *surprised* because you said there was a typo in Israel, and when I went back to look, it was gone"; "I don't use such extensions. draft is not open elsewhere. MacOS autocorrect would not do that trust any reasonable prior puts this with gmail".

What the record holds and lacks: Gmail keeps no draft revision history, and its autocorrect, spelling and grammar changes are logged nowhere a user can see; Gemini Apps Activity logs only features a user invokes. The change is not a spelling correction (spelling would offer "Israel's" or "Israelis"); it replaces a state's name with an article in a sentence about genocide. The test that can fix the mechanism: the original sentence pasted into a fresh draft in the same account and browser, under screen recording, with Gmail's Autocorrect, Spelling and Grammar settings noted.

Settings in effect (Avik's screenshot of Gmail Settings > General, 2026-11-18 07:32 UTC): Grammar suggestions on; Spelling suggestions on; Autocorrect on; Smart Compose writing suggestions on; Smart Compose personalization on. Of these, Autocorrect is the one that changes text without a click.

Reassessment (2026-11-18 07:45 UTC). The draft Avik pasted to Claude reads "(Israels founding upon ethnic cleansing)"; the sentence he later found reads "(implied raised context as the origin of a decades-long genocide)", and the draft had gained whole paragraphs in between: he was revising, and rewrote that parenthetical himself. Whether the one word in the rewritten sentence was typed as "a" or typed as "Israels" and replaced is not settled by the record. Avik: "hm. then this may be a false flag by me. if there's evidence I edited the sentence . I don't recall edits but it was a lot of words you don't remember every detail". The claim stands as unproven, not as false; the reproduction test decides it.
